#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193647} :activity/id #uuid "02255835-1ac4-5d6f-b968-e337f6d42745" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048337 :topic/id #uuid "96874e3b-8b97-5c33-995d-d34a9d3daf21" :topic/math-academy-id 4077} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193647 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193876 :tutorial/content "Recall that $H\\subseteq \\mathbb R^n$ is a **subspace** of $\\mathbb{R}^n$ if and only if it satisfies the following properties:\n\n- *Closure under vector addition:* \n\n$\\qquad$ If $\\mathbf{u}$ and $\\mathbf{v}$ are in $H,$ then their sum $\\mathbf{u}+\\mathbf{v}$ is also in $H.$\n\n- *Closure under scalar multiplication:* \n\n$\\qquad$ If $\\mathbf{u}$ is in $H,$ then $c\\cdot \\mathbf{u}$ is also in $H$ for any scalar number $c.$\n\nIn the vector space $\\mathbb{R}^n,$ there are two **trivial subspaces**.\n\n- One trivial subspace is the set \n\n$$\nH=\\{\\mathbf{0}\\}\n$$\n\n consisting of just the zero vector. This is a subspace because the sum of two zero vectors is the zero vector, and any multiple of the zero vector is the zero vector.\n\n- Another trivial subspace is the set \n\n$$\nH = \\mathbb{R}^n\n$$\n\n consisting of the entire set $\\mathbb{R}^n.$ This is a subspace because the sum of two vectors in $\\mathbb{R}^n$ is in $\\mathbb{R}^n,$ and any multiple of a vector in $\\mathbb{R}^n$ is in $\\mathbb{R}^n.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "01ed4d92-df35-5f3c-aa41-43246f6ea9e9" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6854 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "93a1b04d-d02e-5aae-bd94-6c2a571d80d7" :step/math-academy-id 41649 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193651}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193648 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192780 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192504 :question/id #uuid "32f7b0e6-5a68-52ab-9d86-ea71387cfb77" :question/math-academy-id "e-10975"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e3fe3596-340e-5155-bed3-cf25f5843d70" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186051046}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193312} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193383} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247149} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275420} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317172} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323703} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407336} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407337} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186426532}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Whether Sets Represented Graphically Are Subspaces in Two-Dimensional Space"} :step/id #uuid "f42bb3f4-1e7f-53f5-ad85-ca2e942d30b3" :step/math-academy-id 41650 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193652}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193649 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192781 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192505 :question/id #uuid "554fc7b1-62ca-5189-bda1-712f518ad617" :question/math-academy-id "e-10976"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1094661c-8c7c-5f85-b731-4dadb0aec310" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050367}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193313} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193318} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247150} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247159} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247160} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317380} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323702} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342187} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342188} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342191} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186426533} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186426535} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186434595}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Whether Sets Represented Using Set Notation Are Subspaces"} :step/id #uuid "4c579fcb-af17-53b9-89c4-b7c4d86d6a8f" :step/math-academy-id 41653 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193653}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193650 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192782 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192506 :question/id #uuid "6e80df98-c67c-5f27-a44b-d0bc8f681ac6" :question/math-academy-id "e-10979"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4a8dbbab-2dea-5fff-86f0-3411f5405ee9" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193319} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193384} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247121} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247148} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275441} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275442} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275443} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317349} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186426531} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186426534} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186426536}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Whether Sets Represented Graphically Are Subspaces in Three-Dimensional Space"} :step/id #uuid "68866355-7603-545f-b26a-1a0cee48c958" :step/math-academy-id 41665 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193649}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193651 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193877 :tutorial/content "In $\\mathbb{R}^2,$ we have the following theorem:\n\n*The only non-trivial subspaces in $\\mathbb{R}^2$ are straight lines that pass through the origin.*\n\nFor example, the diagram below shows a subspace in $\\mathbb{R}^2.$\n\n![](images/3c/3c0ea9584ae3229a7930c6ef2b53031770a7190b980b5b595cc233cae29ea49d.png)\n\nLet's now consider a few examples of subsets of $\\mathbb{R}^2$ that are *not* subspaces of $\\mathbb{R}^2$.\n\n![](images/5a/5aac9808e9196653ee54974b495443a95d8913cd0b220a6893acb68ff3b967bb.png)\n\n- Diagram I shows a line that does not pass through the origin. It is *not* a subspace because $\\mathbf v$ belongs to the set while $2\\mathbf v$ does not. So the set is not closed under scalar multiplication.\n\n- Diagram II is not a subspace because $\\mathbf u$ and $\\mathbf v$ belong to the set while $\\mathbf u+\\mathbf v$ does not. So the set is not closed under vector addition.\n\n- Diagram III is not a subspace because $\\mathbf v$ belongs to the set while $-\\mathbf v$ does not. So the set is not closed under scalar multiplication." :tutorial/id #uuid "33c42a7a-a5c2-5b36-a839-4fab6351c9e4" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6857 :tutorial/title "Non-Trivial Subspaces in Two-Dimensional Space"} :step/id #uuid "18e8a405-fc8d-5344-9133-d42b106e46e6" :step/math-academy-id 41670 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193648}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193652 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193878 :tutorial/content "In $\\mathbb{R}^3,$ we have the following theorem:\n\n*The only non-trivial subspaces in $\\mathbb{R}^3$ are straight lines or planes that pass through the origin.*\n\nFor example, the diagram below shows two subspaces in $\\mathbb{R}^3.$\n\n![](images/57/57b6625b607537d1c90e849d6ee6ac8480648aa4ad6e3a357bedc8d8408256ad.png)\n\nOn the other hand, the sets shown in the diagrams below are *not* subspaces in $\\mathbb{R}^3.$\n\n![](images/f4/f4fbaee302e1c25e740f4164841a674243a43197b0f20ddb35d2d41bb4175b68.png)\n\n- Diagram I shows a plane that does not pass through the origin. It is not a subspace because $\\mathbf v$ belongs to the set while $2\\mathbf v$ does not. So, the set is not closed under scalar multiplication.\n\n- Diagram II shows a line that does not pass through the origin. It is not a subspace because $\\mathbf v$ belongs to the set while $2\\mathbf v$ does not. So, the set is not closed under scalar multiplication." :tutorial/id #uuid "8d9802f0-c392-5e37-afb4-72f73451813e" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6861 :tutorial/title "Non-Trivial Subspaces in Three-Dimensional Space"} :step/id #uuid "40d83f58-34e5-5b7b-b300-54ae33e99a59" :step/math-academy-id 41676 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193650}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193653 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193879 :tutorial/content "Lastly, consider the set $\\mathbb{R},$ which belongs to the family of vector spaces $\\mathbb{R}^n$ (when $n=1$). This vector space has only two subspaces, both of which are trivial:\n\n- the zero subspace $\\{0\\},$ which contains only the number $0,$ and\n\n- the whole space $\\mathbb{R}$ (or, formally, $\\mathbb{R}^1$), which contains all the real numbers." :tutorial/id #uuid "fb0671eb-0c5d-59dc-a3f9-cda72b4da034" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6862 :tutorial/title "Final Notes About the Set of Real Numbers"} :step/id #uuid "fda08207-59ef-5481-816d-d4abed842b3a" :step/math-academy-id 41677}] :activity/title "Subspaces of N-Dimensional Space: Geometric Interpretation" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186192920}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046659} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186434597} :activity/id #uuid "888de114-5cc0-5506-ab02-2950778f2cdd" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048337 :topic/id #uuid "96874e3b-8b97-5c33-995d-d34a9d3daf21" :topic/math-academy-id 4077} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186434596 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186434595 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1382095a-ce36-5649-9176-e51b8a45eecf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434589} {:answer/id #uuid "1b5c0bb0-6a86-5a6c-bcaf-1f0fea305db4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434590} {:answer/id #uuid "d03236d8-6be8-5408-b90d-1fcabe487cf1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434591} {:answer/id #uuid "e18c382f-1d1e-5fc7-a402-581360ddc5de" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434592} {:answer/id #uuid "e3d0d900-1221-5e04-8384-9a017c7b2878" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434593}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186434589} :answer-field/id #uuid "b99629cf-233b-5877-9205-658da974228d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434594}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "8f1ed82d-67ec-55b7-826d-30733bc2c88f" :question/math-academy-id "q-35575" :question/problem "Which of the following sets is a subspace of ${R}^{2}?$\n\nI. $A=\\{⟨x,y⟩\\in {R}^{2}:y=0\\}$\n\nII. $B=\\{⟨x,y⟩\\in {R}^{2}:y=3x\\}$\n\nIII. $C=\\{⟨x,y⟩\\in {R}^{2}:y=2\\}$" :question/worked-solution "Let's analyze each of the subsets.\n\n- The set $A$ is a subspace. It represents a line, and a line is a subspace if and only if it passes through the origin. Here, the components of the zero vector $0=⟨0,0⟩$ satisfy the equation $y=0$ since $0=0.$ So, $0$ lies in $A.$\n\n- The set $B$ is a subspace. It represents a line, and a line is a subspace if and only if it passes through the origin. Here, the components of the zero vector $0=⟨0,0⟩$ satisfy the equation $y=3x$ since $0=3\\cdot 0.$ So, $0$ lies in $B.$\n\n- The set $C$ is not a subspace because it does not contain the zero vector. Indeed, $0=⟨0,0⟩$ does not satisfy the condition $y=2$ since $0\\ne 2.$\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""} :step/id #uuid "353deabb-99c1-527a-9354-09b539a6e8de" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186434600}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186434597 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317349 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0ef4ac66-d953-5fba-ac6a-17597fb2834c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315208} {:answer/id #uuid "28317ab6-956f-51f4-9c07-7893dc4262d4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315349} {:answer/id #uuid "6c9c2bd9-cb6c-5ceb-9adb-bb9bf53c2214" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315732} {:answer/id #uuid "8cada019-11c7-5af3-83c1-8512283cb4ef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315902} {:answer/id #uuid "c19aff71-14a7-558f-9b3f-f6060cbbd5ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316205}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186315732} :answer-field/id #uuid "fbb47da4-af36-516e-a2d7-8a35d392e1aa" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317144}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "113a385d-0512-508f-a50c-e45bc26c3c1f" :question/math-academy-id "q-34300" :question/problem "![](images/cc/ccb3b1dc1f175f6f018cb10e47db43a2ac5b4377c565dec4ee389b2a80455203.png)\n\nWhich of the diagrams shown above represent subspaces of ${R}^{2}$ or ${R}^{3}?$" :question/worked-solution "Let's analyze each diagram in turn.\n\n- Diagram I represents a line that does not pass through the origin. But every subspace must contain the origin, so the line in Diagram I is not a subspace of ${R}^{2}.$\n\n- Diagram II is a line that passes through the origin. Therefore, it is a subspace of ${R}^{3}.$\n\n- Diagram III does not represent a subspace. The origin $0=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$ lies outside the subset.\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"II only.\""} :step/id #uuid "63f6444a-ffbe-508f-8985-460e2401c922" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186434596}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186434598 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275442 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "14ca2bb0-45fa-515e-86a8-cce64866690b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272574} {:answer/id #uuid "604d7a59-1fef-523e-bf07-a9f5dd400e7c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273219} {:answer/id #uuid "6afe9e0d-cdbd-5294-ac31-28a3a86f03a6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273310} {:answer/id #uuid "a4a6743f-b156-512b-8812-e7ac6baca636" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273808} {:answer/id #uuid "dfde1b99-be1a-5fba-9ccf-784956f7918d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274365}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186274365} :answer-field/id #uuid "73ce813a-1598-5c11-b720-fcf588d7f65b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274832}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "7ce01e6e-4d84-5a03-940c-8de7146f3c76" :question/math-academy-id "q-158101" :question/problem "![](images/27/2739a0225640ff20cc9678edf8c41ba45ff637b17a40d0322ca792bef1346f06.png)\n\nWhich of the diagrams shown above represent subspaces of ${R}^{3}?$" :question/worked-solution "Let's analyze each diagram in turn.\n\n- Diagram I does not represent a subspace. The origin $0=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$ clearly lies outside the subset.\n\n- Diagram II does not represent a subspace. The origin $0=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$ clearly lies outside the subset.\n\n- Diagram III represents a line passing through the origin. Therefore, it is a subspace of ${R}^{3}.$\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"III only.\""} :step/id #uuid "769529b7-074a-511d-8739-3df09643b9d7"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186434599 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247159 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3d3026ef-0c9f-56f6-9c82-deeb31d5c484" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244850} {:answer/id #uuid "9497acc2-e957-5d08-b023-f36e5c982edf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245613} {:answer/id #uuid "b34b7ad9-ca5d-5d2e-8c18-a98455f051ef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245911} {:answer/id #uuid "b9e6cab8-baf8-54e3-aa8d-7bdaa5531b01" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245973} {:answer/id #uuid "def3c3d6-726e-5345-9da6-7f08cc46817e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246290}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186246290} :answer-field/id #uuid "4c72e079-6317-5a21-9e66-00a4c7adfda0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246673}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "49bd226a-4547-5889-a62c-e6c2912a1520" :question/math-academy-id "q-110760" :question/problem "Which of the following sets is a subspace of ${R}^{3}?$\n\nI. $A=\\{⟨x,y,z⟩\\in {R}^{3}:x-4y=1\\}$\n\nII. $B=\\{⟨x,y,z⟩\\in {R}^{3}:x=-2y\\}$\n\nIII. $C=\\{⟨x,y,z⟩\\in {R}^{3}:{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}<0\\}$" :question/worked-solution "Let's analyze each subset.\n\n- The set $A$ is not a subspace because it does not contain the zero vector. For instance, $0=⟨0,0,0⟩$ does not satisfy the equation $x-4y=1$ since $0-4\\cdot 0=0\\ne 1.$\n\n- The set $B$ is a subspace. It represents a plane, and a plane is a subspace if and only if it passes through the origin. Here, the components of the zero vector $0=⟨0,0,0⟩$ satisfy the equation $x=-2y$ since $0=-2\\cdot 0.$ So $0$ lies in $B.$\n\n- The set $C$ is not a subspace. No point in ${R}^{3}$ satisfies the equation ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}<0.$ Hence, $C=∅.$ The empty set is not a subspace since it does not contain the zero vector.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"II only.\""} :step/id #uuid "c5ce9ac0-bc5b-50bf-a92d-9c79b7832a98" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186434598}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186434600 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342187 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0db4180e-9e56-5615-a261-a06b057afbc7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339254} {:answer/id #uuid "44382309-a210-5068-b421-83edd371f540" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339728} {:answer/id #uuid "705bf110-f45f-5748-8977-a6e4eaab55d9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340124} {:answer/id #uuid "a661de8b-e986-5edb-ae2a-54d4fbff884d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340638} {:answer/id #uuid "ec1d59d6-b1c0-5ab1-b672-2772922e372b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341209}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186340124} :answer-field/id #uuid "b4646991-5d1f-5339-9e5a-2aa1bae21d22" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341696}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "5e93e600-4a01-5ada-842d-9b175b57ee56" :question/math-academy-id "q-74217" :question/problem "Which of the following sets is a subspace of ${R}^{3}?$\n\nI. $A=\\{⟨x,y,z⟩\\in {R}^{3}:1-x-y=0\\}$\n\nII. $B=\\{⟨x,y,z⟩\\in {R}^{3}:{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}\\le 1\\}$\n\nIII. $C=\\{⟨x,y,z⟩\\in {R}^{3}:2x+3y-z=0\\}$" :question/worked-solution "Let's analyze each of the subsets.\n\n- The set $A$ is not a subspace because it does not contain the zero vector. Indeed, $0=⟨0,0,0⟩$ does not satisfy the equation $1-x-y=0$ since $1-0-0\\ne 0.$\n\n- The set $B$ is not a subspace. Indeed, the vector $u=⟨1,0,0⟩$ is in $B$, but $2u=⟨2,0,0⟩$ is not in $B$, since ${2}^{2}+{0}^{2}+{0}^{2}≰1.$ So, $B$ is not closed under scalar multiplication.\n\n- The set $C$ is a subspace. It represents a plane, and a plane is a subspace if and only if it passes through the origin. Here, the components of the zero vector $0=⟨0,0,0⟩$ satisfy the equation $2x+3y-z=0$ since $2\\cdot 0+3\\cdot 0-0=0.$ So $0$ lies in $C.$\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"III only.\""} :step/id #uuid "e64a068c-ae0d-53dd-b1d8-27da858b1eac" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186434599}}] :activity/title "Subspaces of N-Dimensional Space: Geometric Interpretation" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434588}]}
